st 30,000  hectares of trees to be planted each  year until 2050 the UK is woefully  behind that and the current recommend a  of 20,000 hectares a year in 2018 a  little over nine thousand hectares were  planted planting that many trees as well  as boosting active woodland management  would increase the net amount of carbon  trapped in UK forests and woodland to 22  metric tonnes per year by 2050 66  year-old dick Walters has been a local  tree warden in East Lee Hampshire for  the last 30 years he's among volunteers  who've transformed an overgrown woodland  into a thriving forest  he's now passing on his wisdom to  younger generations it's not taught in  school now a lot of children at 10 or 11  don't know where an apple came from I  haven't seen an oak tree and don't  understand a process of nature and what  nature does there our answer for the  future  it's one of 6,000 tree wardens across  the country who are playing a vital role  in ensuring our trees and looked after  but increasing tree cover in the UK by  such a large amount can't be left just  local groups alone it'll need major  projects investment and new forests  created from scratch 25 years ago this  area of the Midlands was mining  territory coal fields left desolate as  the industry declined it's now the  National Forest 200 square miles of  regenerated land thriving with millions  of trees with more being planted every  year a success story in turning  wastelander woodland carbon emitting to  carbon absorbing there are sustainable  Sue Anderson helped plant the first  trees we're creating a whole new forest  here been going 20 odd years we began  with about 6 percent woodland cover and  we're now at 20% and we're heading  through a roundabout third so all around  you you will see young trees but also  trees that are 20 20 25 years  sold beginning to feel like a mature  forest the national forest is living  proof that creating vast new woodlands  can be achieved in a relatively short  space of time 